course: everyday
lesson: 31
title: Safe in the kitchen
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The kitchen is one of the most useful rooms in a home, and also one with the most ways to get hurt: heat, sharp edges, germs and slippery floors.
explain: Most kitchen accidents happen in a moment, so good habits matter more than luck: turn pot handles in, use dry oven mitts, carry knives pointing down and wipe up spills at once.
explain: Germs are a hidden danger. You cannot see the bacteria on raw chicken, but they can make you ill, so you wash your hands with soap and keep raw meat away from food that will not be cooked.
explain: For example, a pot handle sticking out over the edge of the stove can be knocked by an elbow or grabbed by a small child, and boiling water pours down. Turned inward, over the counter, it is out of the way.
explain: A fridge does not kill germs; it keeps food cold so germs grow very slowly. That is why food that is left out too long, or kept too long even in the fridge, can still go bad.
explain: A toddler in Ohio pulled on a saucepan handle sticking out over the edge of the cooker; the hot water poured over him and he needed hospital treatment for burns.
explain: A teenager in Wales cut a bagel while holding it in her palm; the knife slipped straight into her hand and she needed stitches.
fact: Pot and pan handles should be turned inward so nobody can knock or grab them.
fact: Oven mitts must be dry, because heat passes quickly through a wet cloth and can burn your hand.
fact: A knife should be used only with an adult's permission, on a cutting board, cutting away from your fingers.
fact: If a knife falls, step back and let it drop; never try to catch it.
fact: Hands should be washed with soap and water for about 20 seconds before cooking and after touching raw meat or eggs.
fact: Raw meat, fish and eggs can carry bacteria that cause food poisoning, so they need their own board or a well-washed one.
fact: A fridge should be at 40 °F (4 °C) or colder, and a freezer at 0 °F (−18 °C).
fact: Cold slows the growth of bacteria but does not kill them.
fact: Food that goes bad does not always smell or look bad, so smell is not a safe test.
fact: Spills on the floor should be wiped up at once, because wet floors cause slips and falls.
fact: A small burn should be cooled under cool running water for about 20 minutes, not with ice or butter.
fact: Children should cook only with an adult nearby and never leave the stove on when nobody is watching it.
fact: A hot drink can still scald a small child badly fifteen minutes after it was made.
fact: Most kitchen cuts happen when a knife slips towards the hand holding the food.
exercise: Which way should the handle of a pot on the stove point? | Inward, over the stove or counter | Outward, so it is easy to grab quickly | Toward the front edge of the stove | Toward the person who is doing the cooking
exercise: Why must oven mitts be dry? | Heat passes quickly through a wet mitt | Wet mitts are too slippery to hold | Water makes the mitt shrink in the oven | Dry mitts keep the food from cooling
exercise: A knife slips off the counter and falls. What should you do? | Step back and let it fall to the floor | Try to catch it quickly before it lands | Put your foot out to stop it | Grab it quickly by the handle as it drops
exercise: How long should you scrub your hands with soap before cooking? | About 20 seconds | About 2 seconds only | About 2 whole minutes | Only until they are wet
exercise: When must you wash your hands while cooking? | After touching raw meat or raw eggs | Only after you finish eating the meal | Only if your hands look dirty to you | Only after cutting up the vegetables
exercise: What does a fridge do to the bacteria in food? | It slows their growth but does not kill them | It kills all of them after one day in the cold | It makes them grow faster | It turns them into harmless water
exercise: How cold should a home fridge be? | 40 °F (4 °C) or colder | At about room temperature | Just below boiling point | 60 °F (16 °C) or warmer
exercise: Which is the safest way to cut a carrot? | On a board, cutting away from your fingers | In your hand, cutting toward your thumb | On your lap, cutting toward your body | On a plate, holding the carrot up in the air
exercise: A cup of juice spills on the kitchen floor. What should you do? | Wipe it up at once so nobody slips | Leave it until after dinner is over | Step over it carefully and carry on | Cover it with a towel and forget it
exercise: Why should raw chicken not touch salad on the same board? | Bacteria from the raw chicken can get onto the salad | The salad makes the chicken cook unevenly in the pan later | The chicken will lose its flavour | The board will become too slippery
exercise: Is smelling food a safe way to tell if it has gone bad? | No, harmful bacteria often have no smell | Yes, bad food always smells bad | Yes, if it smells fine it is always safe | No, because smelling food spreads germs
exercise: A boy in Ohio touches a hot pan and burns his finger a little. What should he do first? | Hold it under cool running water for a long time | Put an ice cube right on the burn and hold it there | Rub butter on the burn | Wrap it tightly in a dry towel
exercise: Why should a child not cook alone at the stove? | Fires and burns can happen fast and need an adult | Stoves are too heavy for children to switch on | Children are not allowed in kitchens by law | Food cooked by children does not get hot
exercise: Which way should you carry a knife across the kitchen? | Pointing down, held close to your side | Pointing forward in front of you, so you can see it | Pointing up above your head | Tucked loosely under your arm
test: A girl in Leeds sees her little brother reaching up toward the stove, where a pot handle sticks out over the edge. What is the best thing to do? | Turn the handle inward and tell an adult | Pull the pot toward the edge for him | Let him reach it to learn a lesson | Pour the pot out into the sink
test: Which of these is true about a fridge? | Food in it can still go bad, only more slowly | Food in it stays safe forever | It kills germs within a few hours | It makes food fresher than the day it was bought
test: Sam's oven mitt got soaked when he washed the dishes. Why should he not use it to take a hot tray out? | The heat would get through the wet mitt fast | A wet mitt makes the tray heavier | The wet mitt would cool the tray down too fast to eat | The tray would stick to the wet mitt
test: Two friends make sandwiches after cutting raw chicken for the grill. What must they do first? | Wash their hands and use a clean board | Wipe their hands on a dry towel | Rinse the knife in cold water only | Nothing, as long as they are quick
test: Milk in the fridge is past its date but smells fine. Which statement is correct? | A normal smell does not prove it is safe | If it smells fine, it is always safe to drink | Milk can never go bad in a fridge | Smelling it removes the germs
test: What would most likely happen if a pot handle stuck out and someone walked past quickly? | They could knock it and spill hot food | The pot would cool down faster | The handle would bend into place | The stove would turn itself off
test: Mia drops a sharp knife while chopping. Her mum says "Let it go!" Why is that good advice? | Catching a falling knife can cut your hand badly | The floor will clean the knife | Knives always land handle first | A falling knife cannot hurt anyone, so it doesn't matter
test: What is the best first aid for a small burn from a kettle? | Cool running water for about 20 minutes | Ice held on the skin for an hour | A thick layer of butter or oil, rubbed in well | A tight bandage straight away
test: Which habit stops the most falls in a kitchen? | Wiping up spills as soon as they happen | Wearing socks without shoes | Leaving the oven door open | Keeping the floor freshly mopped and wet
test: pan handle sticking out over the stove | Turn it inward | Leave it, it's fine | Point it at the door | Hang a tea towel on it
test: knife's falling off the counter, catch it? | No, step back and let it drop | Yes, catch it quickly | Yes, catch it with both hands together | Kick it away
test: oven glove's a bit wet, ok? | No, heat goes through wet cloth | Yes, wet is cooler on your hands | Yes, it works better wet, it stops burns | Yes, if it's only a little bit damp
test: burnt my finger on the pan | Cool running water for about 20 minutes | Put butter on it | Put ice right on it | Blow on it for a second or two, then carry on
test: little sis is reaching for my hot chocolate on the table | Move it far back out of her reach at once | Let her have a tiny sip | Tell her it is hot and leave it right there | Blow on it so it is safe
test: pan handle's sticking out over the edge, whatever | Turn it in so nobody can knock or grab it | Leave it, it is easier to grab | Hang a towel on it | Turn the heat up so it cooks faster
test: cutting an apple in my hand, it's quicker | Use a board and cut away from your fingers | Cut towards your thumb carefully | Hold it tighter in your palm | Use a blunt knife so it cannot hurt you at all
test: did u hear Noah's little sister grabbed a pan handle off the stove and got burnt really bad | Real lesson: turn pan handles inward so nobody can knock or grab them | Real lesson: little children cannot ever enter a kitchen | It proves pans are only dangerous when boiling | It is gossip, so handle direction does not matter
test: my mate says if u drop a knife u gotta catch it quick before it hits ur foot | Wrong; step back and let a falling knife drop, never try to catch it | He is right, quick hands stop accidents | He is right, if you catch it by the handle and not by the sharp blade | He is right, but only for big knives
test: Ruby got food poisoning after her mum's barbecue, everyone says her mum can't cook, tell people? | No, it is unkind and may be untrue; the lesson is about clean hands and raw meat | Yes, because food poisoning proves it | Yes, so nobody goes to her barbecues | Yes, and say half the guests were sick
exam: Identify the principal reason for which oven mitts must be dry when used. | Heat passes rapidly through wet cloth and may burn the hand | Wet mitts cause the oven to cool too quickly | Dry mitts are more easily washed afterwards | Wet mitts slip off the hands more readily
exam: It being given that a knife is falling from a worktop, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To step back and permit the knife to fall | To attempt to catch the knife by its handle | To place a foot beneath the knife to soften its fall | To catch the knife with a tea towel
exam: Which of the following temperatures is recommended for a domestic refrigerator? | Forty degrees Fahrenheit (four degrees Celsius) or colder | Sixty degrees Fahrenheit (sixteen degrees Celsius) or warmer | Zero degrees Fahrenheit, the temperature of a freezer | Room temperature, provided the door remains closed
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the effect of cold upon bacteria. | Cold retards the growth of bacteria but does not kill them | Cold destroys all bacteria within a few hours | Cold accelerates the growth of bacteria | Cold has no effect of any kind upon bacteria
